\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 California's Gig Worker Law Is Unconstitutional. What's Next?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Conger and Kellen Browning \par \uc2 1,134 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 August 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The law, the result of a $200 million proposition fight last year, ensures that workers like Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  drivers are considered independent contractors.\par \par \uc2 Emboldened by a California election victory that maintained the independence of their drivers last year, gig economy companies like Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  have in recent months accelerated a push for what they call a ''third way'' of working, a classification of independent gig workers who receive limited benefits without gaining employee status.\par \par \uc2 But that plan was upended on Friday evening by a California judge who ruled that the ballot initiative backed by Uber, \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  and other so-called gig economy companies violated the state's Constitution. It was a potential setback for the companies and a victory for labor organizers and drivers who argue they are being treated unfairly.\par \par \uc2 Here is an explanation of this long-simmering fight and what happens next:\par \par \uc2 Wasn't this issue settled in California?\par \par \uc2 Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  have long said their drivers are independent contractors, which allows the companies to avoid the expense of health insurance, unemployment insurance, sick leave and other employment benefits.\par \par \uc2 Some state legislatures, federal officials and legal experts, however, have maintained that drivers are employees under the law, and that Uber and other gig companies owe them the full protections that come with employment.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, California legislators passed a law requiring companies like Uber to employ their drivers. The state attorney general sued Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  to enforce the law, and the companies responded by threatening to leave the state.\par \par \uc2 Uber, \uc2 Lyft\uc2  and \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  poured more than $200 million into a ballot measure, known as Proposition 22, that would allow drivers to remain independent contractors, while companies offered them limited benefits. Prop. 22 was approved in November with about 59 percent of the vote.\par \par \uc2 A coalition of ride-hail drivers and labor groups sued in January, arguing that Prop. 22 is unconstitutional. A month later, the California Supreme Court declined to hear the case, seemingly putting an end to the challenge. But the group refiled its petition in a lower court, leading to last week's ruling.\par \par \uc2 Why did the judge find Prop. 22 unconstitutional?\par \par \uc2 The decision by Judge Frank Roesch of California Superior Court in Alameda County had three main findings.\par \par \uc2 The first was that Prop. 22 carved gig workers out of the pool of employees eligible for workers' compensation in the event of an injury or other workplace incident. But the State Legislature has a right under California's Constitution to set and control workers' compensation.\par \par \uc2 Judge Roesch wrote in his decision that Prop. 22 ''limits the power of a future legislature to define app-based drivers as workers subject to workers' compensation law'' and is therefore unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 Second, Prop. 22 included several unusual provisions designed to prevent the Legislature from making significant changes to the law.\par \par \uc2 The measure requires the Legislature to reach a seven-eighths majority to make any changes to the law, a supermajority that is considered unattainable. It also requires that any changes be ''consistent'' with Prop. 22, blocking the Legislature from drastically altering or reversing the law.\par \par \uc2 If the independent status of drivers was changed, the rest of Prop. 22 would be invalid as well. So if the drivers were declared employees, Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  could back away from the higher wages, private accident insurance and other benefits offered under Prop. 22.\par \par \uc2 Because the workers' compensation issue could not be separated from the rest of Prop. 22, Judge Roesch wrote ''that the entirety of Proposition 22'' could not be enforced.\par \par \uc2 Finally, the judge also took issue with a clause in Prop. 22 that prevents gig workers from unionizing. Prop. 22 said any future law that gave an organization the right to collectively bargain for drivers' benefits, compensation or working conditions would be considered an amendment and would be subject to the seven-eighths majority rule. Judge Roesch found that provision to be unconstitutional because a collective bargaining law ought to be considered ''unrelated legislation.''\par \par \uc2 Who intervened to block Prop. 22?\par \par \uc2 Three ride-hail drivers and one rider are involved in the lawsuit, along with the \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''We're going to keep putting a spotlight on how gig corporations are putting their profits before their workers,'' Michael Robinson, a Lyft driver from Loma Linda, Calif., said in a news conference on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Who is on the other side of the courtroom?\par \par \uc2 Although the lawsuit focuses on how app-based companies treat their workers, the coalition of drivers and labor groups is suing the State of California and the Department of Industrial Relations, which administers workers' compensation.\par \par \uc2 The California attorney general's office is now defending Prop. 22 -- an awkward turn of events, since the attorney general sued Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  before Prop. 22 was approved in an attempt to force the companies to employ their drivers.\par \par \uc2 The gig economy companies can still weigh in. Their coalition, Protect App-Based Drivers and Services, is a respondent in the lawsuit and has said it plans to file an appeal.\par \par \uc2 ''This outrageous decision is an affront to the overwhelming majority of California voters who passed Prop. 22,'' said Geoff Vetter, a spokesman for the coalition. ''We will file an immediate appeal and are confident the Appellate Court will uphold Prop. 22.''\par \par \uc2 What's next?\par \par \uc2 California's attorney general or Protect App-Based Drivers and Services can file an appeal to overturn Judge Roesch's decision. Even an expedited appeal could take several months.\par \par \uc2 For now, gig economy companies might be required to begin paying into workers' compensation funds -- but the companies argue that nothing will change until the appeal is resolved. They also said they had no immediate plans to change how drivers were classified. All of the provisions of Prop. 22 will stay in place until the appeals process is completed, Mr. Vetter said.\par \par \uc2 Stacey Leyton, the lawyer for the drivers, disagreed. ''The Superior Court declared Prop. 22 invalid,'' and drivers ought to be considered employees immediately, she said.\par \par \uc2 The California fight is starting to be repeated in other states. In August, the companies filed for a similar ballot push in Massachusetts, where gig worker treatment is already facing close scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 The S.E.I.U. and other labor activists vowed to keep up their fight and plan to help drivers' organizing and activist efforts.\par \par \uc2 ''We'll continue to support their actions for their demand for basic rights that are afforded to them under current law, reaffirmed to them on Friday,'' said Alma Hern\u225\'00\'E1ndez, the executive director for S.E.I.U. California.\par \par \uc2 Gig workers protest Prop. 22 outside Uber's headquarters last October. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JIM WILSON/THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210824eh8o0003v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\png