\par\par \uc2 The Arts/Cultural Desk; SECTC\par \b \uc2 Theater Chief With Inflated R\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 Quits, Citing Mental Illness\b0\par\par\uc2 By Rebecca J. Ritzel \par \uc2 652 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 August 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Christopher Massimine, whose job as the managing director of the Pioneer Theater Company in Salt Lake City was thrown into doubt after a local television affiliate found he had embellished his r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 with untrue claims, has announced he will resign his post and said that he had long struggled with mental illness.\par \par \uc2 Massimine announced his resignation Monday, shortly after The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  published an article about his career, and the discrepancies and errors on the r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 that had helped him win the position at the Pioneer, the largest professional theater company in Salt Lake City.\par \par \uc2 ''Despite many good things that have happened over the last two years under my direction, effective Aug. 20, 2021, I will resign my position at Pioneer Theater Company in order to address issues in my personal and professional life, stemming from untreated and at times an incorrectly treated mental health condition,'' he said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Massimine said that he had battled with mental illness for his entire life, and that most of his friends and colleagues had not known of his condition. He had gone to the Pioneer Theater from the National Yiddish Theater Folksbiene in New York.\par \par \uc2 Massimine joined the century-old theater in New York in 2012 and rose to become its chief executive, a post from which he led the Folksbiene to produce a highly successful ''Fiddler on the Roof'' in Yiddish in 2018. The show, directed by Joel Grey, enjoyed a sold-out run at Folksbiene's own theater, within the Museum of Jewish Heritage, before transferring in 2019 to Stage 42, one of Off Broadway's largest venues.\par \par \uc2 Massimine, though, switched jobs early in 2019, leaving the Folksbiene to become managing director of the Pioneer Theater Company. Two years into his tenure there, Massimine was accused of embellishing his life story with inaccurate depictions of his theatrical pursuits and side gigs.\par \par \uc2 Salt Lake City's Fox affiliate KSTU-TV reported earlier this year that Massimine did not have a master's degree from \uc2 New York University\uc2 , as asserted on his r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9. The station said his claims to have helped develop popular video games and some major advertising campaigns did not check out.\par \par \uc2 And, though he said he had received a national arts advocacy award -- and released a picture of himself wearing the medal -- the bestowing organization does not appear to exist.\par \par \uc2 Massimine, whose representatives had denied some of the accusations that he had misrepresented his accomplishments, acknowledged Monday night that there had been ''errors'' in his r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9.\par \par \uc2 ''Local and national news outlets have reported this year that I misrepresented my work history on my r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9, in press releases and interviews, both prior to accepting the P.T.C. position and during my tenure here,'' he said in the statement. ''There is a fair amount of truth within the reporting, withstanding discrepancies. Regardless, I take responsibility for errors in my r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 but stand by my work product throughout my career.''\par \par \uc2 Massimine's embellishments extended beyond what the TV station had reported, to include claims that he was born in Italy and was once a full-time employee of the Dramatists Guild.\par \par \uc2 Before the resignation, Chris Nelson, the Utah university's director of communications, had acknowledged that some ''misinformation'' had been found on Massimine's r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 and that his position was put ''under review.''\par \par \uc2 In remarks before the resignation, Massimine's wife, Maggie, had said that her husband was on family medical leave and not available for interviews but she did not discuss his mental state. A spokesman for Massimine, Michael Deaver, had said that some of the discrepancies might have been attributable to misunderstandings on matters such as his client's work on ad campaigns, where he had been employed by a subcontractor.\par \par \uc2 Christopher Massimine in 2017. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JOHN LAMPARSKI/GETTY IMAGES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210818eh8i00045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
