\par\par \uc2 The Arts/Cultural Desk; SECTC\par \b \uc2 Director Of Theater Faces Doubts Over R\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9\b0\par\par\uc2 By Rebecca J. Ritzel \par \uc2 1,983 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 August 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Christopher Massimine found success as a theater executive in New York and Utah, but resigned after facing questions about errors on his r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9, saying he had mental illness.\par \par \uc2 Christopher Massimine, whose job as the managing director of the Pioneer Theater Company in Salt Lake City was thrown into doubt after a local television affiliate found that he had embellished his r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 with untrue claims, announced Monday that he would resign his post and said that he had long struggled with mental illness.\par \par \uc2 Massimine announced his resignation shortly after The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  published an article about his career, and the discrepancies and errors on the r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 that had helped him win the position at the Pioneer, the largest professional theater company in Salt Lake City.\par \par \uc2 ''Despite many good things that have happened over the last two years under my direction, effective Aug. 20, 2021, I will resign my position at Pioneer Theater Company in order to address issues in my personal and professional life, stemming from untreated and at times an incorrectly treated mental health condition,'' he said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Massimine, who said that he had battled with mental illness for his entire life, and that most of his friends and colleagues had not known of his condition, had come to the Pioneer Theater from the National Yiddish Theater Folksbiene in New York.\par \par \uc2 Massimine was something of an unusual choice to lead the Folksbiene, a small nonprofit with a niche audience.\par \par \uc2 In 2012, when he became an executive with the century-old theater that produces shows for a largely older audience, he was a 26-year-old Italian American Catholic with limited experience as a theatrical administrator and even less with Yiddish.\par \par \uc2 But when he left seven years later, the Folksbiene's ''Fiddler on the Roof,'' directed by Joel Grey, was moving from its own theater, within the Museum of Jewish Heritage, to Stage 42, one of Off Broadway's largest venues. The show had already enjoyed a sold-out run at the museum, and the theater's revenue had more than doubled in a year to nearly $5 million.\par \par \uc2 ''He was smart, dedicated, motivated, professional and always a pleasure to deal with,'' said Ron Lasko, a publicist who worked with Massimine at the Folksbiene.\par \par \uc2 To the surprise of many, though, Massimine did not stick around to celebrate the successful transfer of ''Fiddler'' to a new theater. He instead left the Folksbiene in early 2019 and soon accepted a job as the managing director of the Pioneer Theater Company.\par \par \uc2 ''Chris has a proven track record of success,'' Dan Reed, a vice president with \uc2 the University of Utah\uc2 , which oversees the professional theater on its campus, said at the time of Massimine's appointment.\par \par \uc2 But two years into his tenure there, Massimine was accused of embellishing his life story with wildly inaccurate depictions of his theatrical pursuits and side gigs.\par \par \uc2 Working from public records and tips, Salt Lake City's Fox affiliate KSTU-TV reported earlier this year that Massimine did not have a master's degree from \uc2 New York University\uc2 , as asserted on his r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9. The station said his claims to have helped develop popular video games and some major advertising campaigns did not check out.\par \par \uc2 And, though he said he had received a national arts advocacy award -- and released a picture of himself wearing the medal -- the bestowing organization does not appear to exist.\par \par \uc2 Adam Herbets, a reporter for the television station, said his sources included people who had seen Massimine's r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 and found it ''unbelievable.''\par \par \uc2 ''And, you know,'' he continued, ''unbelievable sometimes has a positive connotation and sometimes has a negative connotation. In this case it's literally not believable.''\par \par \uc2 Massimine, whose representatives had denied some of the accusations that he had misrepresented his accomplishments, acknowledged Monday night that there had been ''errors'' in his r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9.\par \par \uc2 ''Local and national news outlets have reported this year that I misrepresented my work history on my r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9, in press releases and interviews, both prior to accepting the P.T.C. position and during my tenure here,'' he said in the statement. ''There is a fair amount of truth within the reporting, withstanding discrepancies. Regardless, I take responsibility for errors in my r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 but stand by my work product throughout my career.''\par \par \uc2 As it turns out, Massimine's embellishments extended beyond what the TV station had reported, to include claims that he was born in Italy and was once a full-time employee of the Dramatists Guild.\par \par \uc2 Before the resignation, Chris Nelson, the Utah university's director of communications, had acknowledged that some ''misinformation'' had been found on Massimine's r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 and that his position was put ''under review.''\par \par \uc2 In prior remarks, his wife, Maggie Massimine, had said that her husband was on family medical leave and not available for interviews. A spokesman for Massimine, Michael Deaver, had said that some of the discrepancies might have been attributable to misunderstandings on matters such as his client's work on ad campaigns, where he had been employed by a subcontractor.\par \par \uc2 Maggie Massimine had denied that her husband had exaggerated or misled people, but she did not directly discuss his mental state and said she could not address some of the discrepancies.\par \par \uc2 ''Our side of the story has not been told,'' she said in an interview several weeks ago. ''I really wish I could say more.''\par \par \uc2 At N.Y.U., Massimine earned a bachelor's degree in dramatic literature in 2007, a university spokesman said, after three years of study. Maggie Massimine said her husband thought he had earned both a master's and a bachelor's degree, until KSTU reported he had not. ''He was as surprised as everyone else,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Massimine's profile on \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 , the professional networking site, reports that during his college years he also served for more than 18 months as ''publications manager and creative affairs coordinator'' for the Dramatists Guild, the national trade association for playwrights, composers and others.\par \par \uc2 However, \uc2 Tari Stratton\uc2 , director of education for the guild, said it seems Massimine spent only four months there as an unpaid intern. ''We do not have any records indicating Mr. Massimine held any paid positions with the guild or had any title other than intern,'' she wrote in an email.\par \par \uc2 Massimine did serve in a number of roles with theatrical organizations before joining the staff of the Flea, a small, scrappy New York theater, in 2011. The following year he was hired by the Folksbiene and was promoted to chief executive in 2016.\par \par \uc2 At the Yiddish theater, framed letters from Hal Prince, the legendary Broadway producer and director, hung in Massimine's office. He counted Manny Azenberg, a producer and eight-time Tony Award winner, among his mentors, and appeared poised to continue advancing through the ranks of Manhattan's theater ecosystem.\par \par \uc2 Bruce Cohen, a retired publicist who worked with the Folksbiene to promote its Drama Desk-nominated operetta ''The Golden Bride,'' said Massimine was ''a very sweet man'' capable of deftly navigating tempestuous artist egos.\par \par \uc2 Beck Lee, who served as a publicist for the Folksbiene during much of Massimine's tenure, described him as an ambitious hard worker.\par \par \uc2 ''He did a great deal to raise the profile of the company,'' Lee said, ''and was sometimes prone to exaggeration, which I have learned is typically a tool of impresarios and showmen. If anything I thought he was a 21st-century version of a David Merrick, happily pushing his shows to the public and the press with bluster.''\par \par \uc2 Certainly there were issues with a 2018 profile of Massimine that ran in The Daily Beast under the headline, ''Meet Christopher Massimine, the 'Nice Goy' Running the National Yiddish Theatre.''\par \par \uc2 The piece, based on an interview with Massimine, reported he had come to the United States as an infant from Italy and had appeared on Broadway as a child in shows like ''Beauty and the Beast'' and ''Les Mis\u233\'00\'E9rables.'' But he was born in New Jersey and there are no records of him performing in either show, according to the Broadway League's database, which is widely viewed as authoritative.\par \par \uc2 Maggie Massimine said her husband had not been born in Italy and had requested a correction from The Daily Beast, a contention that the website recently took issue with.\par \par \uc2 ''Our editorial staff has no record of any request from Mr. Massimine for a correction to his profile,'' a Daily Beast spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 Despite his success in leading the Folksbiene, the circumstances under which Massimine left the theater are not clear, and its executive director declined to comment. Beck Lee, the former publicist for the theater, said that he was told by theater officials that Massimine was asked to leave after having invested theater funds in an unrelated production without authorization.\par \par \uc2 ''He was given the opportunity to admit his behavior, and leave without further incident,'' Lee said.\par \par \uc2 A second person with knowledge of the dispute agreed that Massimine had left after an issue over an investment.\par \par \uc2 But Maggie Massimine denied there had been any problem like that, and noted that her husband had been invited back to attend the opening of ''Fiddler'' at Stage 42 in February 2019.\par \par \uc2 In Utah, Massimine was hired at a salary of $152,000 to run a theater with nearly a $5 million operating budget. The school had paid a search firm, Management Consultants for the Arts, nearly $36,000 to recommend candidates.\par \par \uc2 ''That r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 was so extraordinary that it probably intoxicated people,'' said Brant Pope, chair of the drama department at the University of Texas at Austin and past president of the University Resident Theater Association. ''It probably blurred their vision.''\par \par \uc2 Maggie Massimine said her husband had heard of the Utah job through his relationship with Azenberg, the producer who has been an influential backer of the Yiddish theater. Azenberg's daughter, Karen, a former Broadway stage manager, has served as the artistic director of the Pioneer Theater since 2012.\par \par \uc2 ''The search committee was looking for a managing director who would help this theater grow and would support my desire to develop new musicals,'' Karen Azenberg wrote in an email.\par \par \uc2 In Utah, Massimine continued to promote his own accomplishments. Two years ago, using information he provided, his new theater put out a news release stating he had been named ''Humanitarian of the Year'' by the National Performing Arts Action Association and would be honored at a reception in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Jenny Thomas, a spokesman for the Association of Performing Arts Professionals, a Washington-based advocacy group, said that neither she nor several other colleagues who work for similar nonprofits have heard of the National Performing Arts Action Association.\par \par \uc2 No organization by that name has a website or is mentioned by news outlets aside from those that picked up the news release from the theater.\par \par \uc2 But Massimine traveled to Washington in January 2020, purportedly to pick up the award, and later billed the university nearly $800 for his expenses. An image of him on the trip, supposedly taken at the White House and wearing a medal, was credited to the fictitious National Performing Arts Action Association and appeared two months later on a website with an article about Massimine's relationship with his mother.\par \par \uc2 The writer of the piece said the photograph, caption and credit information were provided by Massimine.\par \par \uc2 Joseph Berger contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Christopher Massimine, top, helped to revitalize the Folksbiene, the national Yiddish theater company. Above, ''Fiddler on the Roof'' in Yiddish was so popular at its home theater that it later moved to an Off Broadway theater. Below, a photograph that seems to show Massimine at the White House, but the organization said to have taken the photo and bestowed an award does not appear to exist. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY JOHN LAMPARSKI/GETTY IMAGES; SARA KRULWICH/THE NEW YORK TIMES) (C4) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210819eh8h0000d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
