\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Google\uc2  Announces Vaccine Mandate for Workers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kellen Browning, Erin Woo and Kate Conger \par \uc2 553 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 July 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The tech giant joins other companies that have readjusted their plans as the more contagious Delta variant of the coronavirus sweeps through the country.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  said Wednesday that it would require employees who returned to the company's offices to be vaccinated against the coronavirus. It also said it would push back its official return-to-office date to mid-October from September, joining a host of other companies whose plans have been scrambled in recent days by the spread of the highly contagious Delta variant.\par \par \uc2 Sundar Pichai\uc2 , the chief executive of Alphabet, Google's parent company, announced the news in a note to employees, which was reviewed by The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Getting vaccinated is one of the most important ways to keep ourselves and our communities healthy in the months ahead,'' Mr. Pichai wrote. He added that the vaccine mandate would apply to U.S. office locations ''in the coming weeks'' and to other regions ''in the coming months.''\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  has more than 144,000 employees globally. A Google spokeswoman said the company did not have any current vaccination rates to share, though Mr. Pichai wrote that it was ''encouraging to see very high vaccination rates'' among employees in places where vaccines were widely available.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Pichai also said in the note that \uc2 Google\uc2 's voluntary work-from-home policy was being extended through Oct. 18. Previously, employees had been planning to return in September, though no specific date had been set.\par \par \uc2 ''We recognize that many Googlers are seeing spikes in their communities caused by the Delta variant and are concerned about returning to the office,'' Mr. Pichai wrote. ''This extension will allow us time to ramp back into work while providing flexibility for those who need it.''\par \par \uc2 The decision followed a similar announcement from Apple, which said last week that it would push back to October, from September, the date by which employees would need to return to its offices.\par \par \uc2 The companies are among many in tech that are changing their office plans as coronavirus cases spike. \uc2 Lyft\uc2  said on Wednesday that it would not require employees to return to the office until February, while \uc2 Twitter\uc2  said it would close its newly reopened offices in San Francisco and New York and indefinitely postpone other reopening plans.\par \par \uc2 Some \uc2 Google\uc2  employees have been returning to work in the office on a voluntary basis. In California, as the Delta variant of the coronavirus has surged, workers began donning masks in \uc2 Google\uc2  offices again.\par \par \uc2 Silicon Valley tech companies like \uc2 Google\uc2  led the push to remote work in the beginning of the pandemic, but \uc2 Google\uc2  has not fully pivoted away from office work, and it has said it expects most employees to eventually return to in-person work at least three days a week.\par \par \uc2 The company said in March that it would spend $1 billion on California developments this year, including two office complexes in Mountain View. It is also building a 7.3 million-square-foot office space in San Jose.\par \par \uc2 Google's campus in Mountain View, Calif. The company will delay its return to office and mandate vaccines for its workers. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Christie Hemm Klok for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210729eh7t0003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
