\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Unions in New York Bristle After \uc2 de Blasio\uc2  Issues Testing Mandate\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emma G. Fitzsimmons and Ashley Wong \par \uc2 1,697 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 July 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Municipal union leaders bristled at Mayor \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2 's announcement that workers must get vaccinated or tested weekly, while city workers had mixed reactions.\par \par \uc2 Mayor \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2 's declaration on Monday that more than 300,000 municipal workers in New York City must get vaccinated against the coronavirus or agree to weekly testing was an unwelcome surprise to many of the city's municipal unions.\par \par \uc2 Unions representing a diverse city work force of firefighters and paramedics have come out against the mayor's mandate.\par \par \uc2 Some unions have made demands: Exemptions for workers who have antibodies after recovering from Covid-19; workplace testing paid for by the city; overtime for workers who get tested outside work.\par \par \uc2 And just about every major union has argued that the mayor cannot unilaterally impose the mandate without first negotiating with labor leaders.\par \par \uc2 ''The unions are really, really aggravated that the mayor sprung this on everybody,'' said \uc2 Harry Nespoli\uc2 , the president of the sanitation workers' union.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Mr. de Blasio\uc2 , a Democrat in his final year in office, expressed confidence on Tuesday that the city could legally require vaccination or testing for its workers, and that his administration would sort out how to implement the mandate with union leadership.\par \par \uc2 ''We're quite clear that we're procedurally in a strong place,'' \uc2 Mr. de Blasio\uc2  said. ''We have the right as an employer to guarantee the health and safety of our employees and everyone they serve.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. de Blasio\uc2  said the mandate for city workers -- starting Sept. 13, when schools reopen -- was necessary to combat a troubling rise of cases as the contagious Delta variant spreads in the city. Officials in California and at the Department of Veterans Affairs also moved to vaccinate government workers.\par \par \uc2 The opposition from unions -- based in part on a general reluctance to force members, many of whom are Black and Latino, to get the vaccine -- is more rooted in the logistics of offering vaccines or weekly tests, and the discipline for those who do not comply. For now, it seems unlikely that it could lead to lawsuits or strikes.\par \par \uc2 The threat to public workers who are not vaccinated was reinforced on Tuesday when the city's police commissioner, \uc2 Dermot Shea\uc2 , said that five unvaccinated employees of the Police Department were in the hospital with the virus.\par \par \uc2 ''When you look at who is getting sick right now, every story I hear anecdotally, it's somebody that is not vaccinated,'' Mr. Shea said in a television interview on NY1. ''God forbid, you put yourself or your loved ones or anyone else at risk. It's the right thing to do.''\par \par \uc2 The Police Department appears to have one of the lowest vaccination rates among city agencies. The department has administered the vaccine to 43 percent of workers, though other officers have likely received the vaccine on their own.\par \par \uc2 The city's largest police union, the Police Benevolent Association, has so far declined to comment on the new mandate. Other unions have made their opposition clear: The firefighters' union said the city should allow members who test positive for antibodies to receive exemptions from the mandate. The paramedics' union said the city was disregarding its members' civil liberties, and asked for overtime if workers must get tested outside of work hours.\par \par \uc2 ''You're going to have people that are going to resent it,'' Mr. Nespoli said, adding that his sanitation workers -- many of whom got sick as they kept working during the pandemic -- took specific umbrage at the mayor's threat that those who did not comply would be put on leave without pay.\par \par \uc2 ''I was out in one of my garages yesterday and they're saying, 'What's going on with this -- if you don't get the shot, they're going to send you home?''' Mr. Nespoli said.\par \par \uc2 Oren Barzilay, the president of a union of emergency medical workers that opposes the mandate, said that some of his 4,300 members have expressed concerns about side effects or complications from the vaccine and about the \uc2 U.S. Food and Drug Administration\uc2  not yet giving full approval to any coronavirus vaccine.\par \par \uc2 ''There's so many questions unanswered,'' he said. ''How's it going to be implemented? How are we going to get them tested? What happens to us if we have a side effect? It was just a blanket statement without any action plan.''\par \par \uc2 City workers had mixed reactions. Carlotta Pope, a high school English teacher in Brooklyn, is not yet vaccinated and is waiting until she can accompany her mother, who remains skeptical of the vaccine. Ms. Pope said she found the mayor's announcement ''bully-ish,'' but she has no problem being tested weekly and thinks all employees should be subject to frequent testing.\par \par \uc2 ''That may seem tedious,'' she said, ''but it still gives people a partial choice, to not back people into a corner'' on vaccines.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm happy to go back into the building regardless,'' she added.\par \par \uc2 Stiven Taveras, 32, a \uc2 New York City Housing Authority\uc2  employee, said he had not heard of the city's new rules as he collected garbage in Upper Manhattan on Tuesday. Though he wants to get vaccinated, he has not done it yet because he's ''too lazy,'' especially after working 10-hour days.\par \par \uc2 Now, he thinks he and other workers will get vaccinated by September to avoid weekly tests.\par \par \uc2 ''If you show both options, people are going to choose the easy one,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Timmy, a municipal worker who declined to give his last name, said he has been ''wary'' about getting the vaccine and worried about complications from the shot.\par \par \uc2 ''I didn't take it and I'm not going to take it,'' he said, adding that city workers should be able to decide for themselves whether they want to get vaccinated, and the prospect of weekly testing would not change his mind.\par \par \uc2 ''I'll take a test every day if I have to,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Leaders of the city's largest municipal union, District Council 37, had insisted on Monday that the new rules had to be part of collective bargaining negotiations, emphasizing that New York was still a ''union town.''\par \par \uc2 But after District Council 37's executive director, Henry Garrido, met with Dean Fuleihan, \uc2 Mr. de Blasio\uc2 's deputy mayor, on Monday, the union leader said he believed they could figure out the details.\par \par \uc2 ''We're in support of vaccines -- we're in support of testing,'' Mr. Garrido said in an interview on Tuesday. ''We simply feel that we need to negotiate the impact.''\par \par \uc2 Officials still need to sort out details like how to handle scheduling for people who work in the field and need to get tested or who work alone and cannot leave their post unattended, and how to accommodate workers who are allergic to vaccines, Mr. Garrido said.\par \par \uc2 Gregory Floyd\uc2 , the president of a major union that represents public housing workers, said he supports a vaccine requirement for city workers, but he was frustrated by \uc2 Mr. de Blasio\uc2 's hasty announcement, and his members had concerns about the details.\par \par \uc2 ''The mayor is doing a responsible thing by telling us all we have to get vaccinated or get tested every week, but he can't do a responsible thing in a rational or reasonable way,'' Mr. Floyd said. ''He could have had meetings with all of the unions, set the policy, set the tone, answered questions and rolled this out in a cohesive way.''\par \par \uc2 John Samuelsen\uc2 , the president of the transit workers' union, said that his members have ''diverse ideas about whether or not to get the vaccine.'' He has heard from workers who already had Covid-19 and do not believe they need to get vaccinated. \uc2 The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  has said that people should get vaccinated even if they already had Covid-19 because experts do not know how long protection lasts after someone recovers.\par \par \uc2 ''There are transit workers that have religious reasons, health reasons -- it comes from several different angles,'' Mr. Samuelsen said.\par \par \uc2 The unions representing teachers and principals, who have sharply criticized \uc2 Mr. de Blasio\uc2  in the past, appeared to be cooperative. Mark Cannizzaro, president of the principals' union, said that the changes would be ''minor'' for his members and that one positive development is that vaccinated principals would no longer have to submit to weekly testing.\par \par \uc2 Michael Skelly, a spokesman for the Correction Officers' Benevolent Association, which represents New York City's jail officers, said that any required testing should be paid for by the city. His members also appear to have one of the lowest vaccination rates -- the city said that only 33 percent of correction employees had been vaccinated at sites in the city as of last week.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Skelly said that many of the union's members live outside the city and were more likely to have been vaccinated at sites there. He said that members were reluctant to comment on the city's decision or their own vaccination statuses.\par \par \uc2 ''People have a variety of experiences with this, and it's very personal for them,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Dee Torres, 60, a clerical associate for the city, said she had gotten vaccinated and wanted all municipal employees to do the same. But she did not believe that weekly testing would pressure her unvaccinated colleagues to change their minds.\par \par \uc2 ''People are still stuck,'' she said. ''I have co-workers who say they don't need the vaccine.''\par \par \uc2 Reporting was contributed by Jonah E. Bromwich, Troy Closson, Michael Gold, Amanda Rosa, Dana Rubinstein and Eliza Shapiro\par \par \uc2 Reporting was contributed by Jonah E. Bromwich, Troy Closson, Michael Gold, Amanda Rosa, Dana Rubinstein and Eliza Shapiro\par \par \uc2 The leader of the union representing sanitation workers said labor leaders were ''really aggravated'' by the suddenness of the mayor's announcement, which presents logistical problems. Right, Julio Escribano received a shot from Megan O'Neill at the Family Life Academy Charter School in the Bronx on Tuesday. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY JEENAH MOON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; JAMES ESTRIN/THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210728eh7s0004g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
