\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Frito-Lay Workers End Strike After Deal\b0\par\par\uc2 By Maria Cramer \par \uc2 910 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 July 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The workers at a \uc2 Frito-Lay\uc2  plant in Topeka went on strike this month to protest forced overtime and so-called suicide shifts, which they said left little time to sleep or see family members.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of \uc2 Frito-Lay\uc2  employees ratified a contract on Saturday, ending a nearly three-week strike over forced overtime and long hours that many workers said had pushed them past the point of exhaustion, union officials said.\par \par \uc2 The agreement, which was ratified in a vote that one union official described as close, puts an end to what workers at the \uc2 Frito-Lay\uc2  plant in Topeka, Kan., call ''suicide shifts'' -- back-to-back 12-hour shifts with only an eight-hour break in between.\par \par \uc2 ''The outcome of this strike was a testament to the tenacity and grit of the \uc2 Frito-Lay\uc2  workers in Topeka,'' Anthony Shelton, international president of the \uc2 Bakery, Confectionery, Tobacco Workers and Grain Millers International Union\uc2 , which represents the employees who are members of Local 218, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The workers in Local 218 said they went on strike because the company had refused to address their concerns about the shifts, which they said took away time with their families and did not even allow enough time to get a full night's sleep.\par \par \uc2 Workers had said \uc2 Frito-Lay\uc2  could easily address the grueling shifts without hurting the company's bottom line. \uc2 Frito-Lay\uc2  is a division of \uc2 PepsiCo\uc2 , which recently reported a huge jump in revenue in its drinks and snacks businesses.\par \par \uc2 Paul Klemme, a chief shop steward who has worked at the Topeka plant for nine years, said he had once worked three months straight without a day off.\par \par \uc2 ''I missed a lot of time with my children when they were in high school because of the shift that I worked and the hours that I worked,'' he said. ''It's physically draining.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Klemme said the new contract guarantees one day off a week for workers, does away with forcing workers to take the ''suicide shifts'' and increases wages. He declined to provide precise numbers because he said he was not authorized to give that information. Corrina Christensen, a spokeswoman for the international union, declined to provide additional details of the agreement or say how the membership voted.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, \uc2 Frito-Lay\uc2  said the new two-year contract includes a 4 percent wage increase for all job classifications and ''additional opportunities for the union to have input into staffing and overtime.''\par \par \uc2 The company said it believed its approach to resolving the strike ''demonstrates how we listen to our employees, and when concerns are raised, they are taken seriously and addressed.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Klemme said he would have liked to have seen better wage increases for the employees. Some departments had not had an increase in hourly pay of more than 20 to 40 cents in the past six to eight years, he said.\par \par \uc2 ''I think it could have been better, but I think the time off for people is worth it,'' Mr. Klemme said.\par \par \uc2 One warehouse worker who has been at the plant for roughly two decades said there was ''more disappointment than happiness'' with the contract. The man, who spoke on the condition of anonymity, fearful of retaliation from the company, said that many workers who crossed the picket line or voted to approve the contract did so out of need.\par \par \uc2 ''A lot of people had to vote yes because they were running out of money and didn't have insurance,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 The food and beverage giant, which is home to Pepsi, Mountain Dew and Doritos, said this month that its net revenue in the quarter surged by 20.5 percent, to $19.2 billion, from a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Shelton said that more than 600 union members had picketed and marched over the past 20 days.\par \par \uc2 The workers at the Topeka plant ''have shown the world that union working people can stand up against the largest food companies in the world and claim victory for themselves, their families and their communities,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 The company had tried to push back on the union's complaints about the long shifts.\par \par \uc2 It said that out of about 850 employees in Topeka, only 20 averaged over 60 hours per week.\par \par \uc2 The company said its records indicated that 19 employees worked 84 hours in a given workweek this year, and 16 of those had volunteered for overtime. Only three of the 19 had been required to work, the company said.\par \par \uc2 The company also said it was continuously hiring new part-time and full-time workers in Topeka. The plant made 263 hires last year and has hired 209 workers this year.\par \par \uc2 Frito-Lay\uc2  employs more than 66,000 people, and the strike is the first at one of its plants in three decades, according to the company.\par \par \uc2 It was the first time workers at the plant in Topeka went on strike, Mr. Klemme said.\par \par \uc2 Of approximately 850 manufacturing and warehouse employees who are part of the bargaining unit on strike, about 300 continued to work through the strike, the company said.\par \par \uc2 The new contract sets hourly wages at $19.09 to $38.40, according to the company.\par \par \uc2 Frito-Lay employees picketed outside the Topeka, Kan., plant on July 12. The strike lasted nearly three weeks. (PHOTOGRAPH BY EVERT NELSON/THE TOPEKA CAPITAL-JOURNAL, VIA ASSOCIATED PRESS) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210726eh7q0003m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
