\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Virus Briefing\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jack Nicas, Mike Isaac, Nicole Sperling and Niraj Chokshi \par \uc2 717 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 July 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TECH\par \par \uc2 Apple Delays Return to Office Amid Surge of Delta Variant\par \par \uc2 Apple pushed back its return-to-office plans by at least a month in response to the recent surge in coronavirus cases, which has been fueled by the spread of the Delta variant.\par \par \uc2 The company told employees on Monday that they are now expected to return to the office as early as Oct. 1 instead of early September. The company said that the date could shift further depending on the spread of the virus, and that it would give employees at least a month's notice before they are expected to return, according to an email Apple sent to employees, which was viewed by The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''As the situation continues to evolve, we're committed to the same measured approach that we have taken all along,'' the email said.\par \par \uc2 Some employees, such as those who build hardware, have already returned to Apple's offices. At the beginning of the pandemic, Apple closed many of its retail stores, but those have since reopened. Apple's return-to-work policies apply to all of its offices, including those in California, Texas and New York.\par \par \uc2 Apple declined to comment further. The company had 147,000 full-time employees as of September. Bloomberg earlier reported the changed return-to-office date.\par \par \uc2 Throughout the pandemic, Silicon Valley has been at the forefront of the trend toward remote work, with tech companies like \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and \uc2 Facebook\uc2  among the first to order their employees to work from home in early 2020. Many tech companies also eventually decided to make remote work permanent.\par \par \uc2 But Apple has been more resistant to lose its in-person office culture, which has caused some friction among employees who want to continue working from home.\par \par \uc2 JACK NICAS and MIKE ISAAC\par \par \uc2 Entertainment\par \par \uc2 Hollywood Studios Can Require Vaccines for Everyone on Set\par \par \uc2 Hollywood's major unions agreed Monday night to a short-term plan that would allow studios to require everyone on a production set to be vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 The agreement, which will be in effect through the end of September, will also relax some pandemic protocols on production sets, even as the Delta variant climbs and Los Angeles County puts a mask mandate for indoor settings back in place. Studios will be able to decrease the rate of regular coronavirus testing and loosen mask mandates in outdoor settings.\par \par \uc2 The arrangement was agreed on by the \uc2 Directors Guild of America\uc2 , the International Alliance of Theatrical Stage Employees, \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2 , the \uc2 Screen Actors Guild-American Federation of Television and Radio Artists\uc2 , along with the studios as represented by the Alliance of Motion Pictures and Television Producers.\par \par \uc2 The parties said they would continue monitoring Covid-19 developments and ''will consider further modifications.'' NICOLE SPERLING\par \par \uc2 Airlines\par \par \uc2 United Posts Quarterly Loss; Expects Second-Half Profits\par \par \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  announced Tuesday that it lost $434 million during the three months that ended in June, but said that it fared better than anticipated during the quarter and that it expected to turn a profit in the second half of the year. The airline reported revenue of $5.5 billion in the quarter, about half of what it collected in the same quarter in 2019.\par \par \uc2 United said lucrative corporate and international travel was recovering faster than it had forecast. The airline expects a pretax profit in the third quarter, which would be its first since late 2019. United also projected that capacity in that quarter, which ends in September, would be down about 26 percent, compared with the same quarter two years ago.\par \par \uc2 The upbeat tone mirrors that of United's peers. Last week, \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2  reported a profit of $652 million -- lifted by federal stimulus funds -- for the three months that ended in June, its first profitable quarter since the pandemic began. \uc2 American Airlines\uc2 , which is expected to report earnings on Thursday, previewed its financial results last week, saying it may report a slight profit for the quarter.\par \par \uc2 The industry has enjoyed a boom in summer travel within the United States, fueled by widespread coronavirus vaccinations in the spring. Airline stocks fell Monday on fears that the Delta variant threatened that recovery, but have since rebounded. NIRAJ CHOKSHI\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210721eh7l0003q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
