\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Deal Reached With Union At New Yorker\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Robertson \par \uc2 654 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 June 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast, the venerable magazine's parent company, agreed to a minimum salary of $60,000 by April 2023, the union said. The deal also covers workers at Ars Technica and Pitchfork.\par \par \uc2 After more than two years of talks, an evening protest outside Anna Wintour's townhouse in Greenwich Village and the threat of a strike, a group of union employees at The New Yorker and two other publications has reached a deal with their parent company, Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast.\par \par \uc2 The New Yorker Union and the unions representing two other Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast publications, Ars Technica and Pitchfork, came to an agreement with the company on Wednesday after bargaining together for higher wages and improved health benefits, among other demands.\par \par \uc2 ''These landmark agreements, which will go before members for a ratification vote in the coming weeks, will inaugurate a new era of equity, transparency and accountability at The New Yorker, Pitchfork, Ars Technica and Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast at large,'' the unions for the three publications said in a joint statement.\par \par \uc2 The three unions are affiliated with the NewsGuild, which also represents employees at The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  and other media organizations.\par \par \uc2 During talks, The New Yorker Union demanded a base salary of $60,000, saying that some workers in the union made as little as $42,000 annually. The New Yorker Union is made up of fact checkers, copy editors and other editorial workers, but does not include the magazine's staff writers.\par \par \uc2 The deal with Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast includes base pay of $55,000 for employees at all three unions, rising to $60,000 by April 2023. Under the agreement, many employees at the three publications will receive wage increases of at least 10 percent, the unions said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The agreement includes a cap on increases for health care costs and defined working hours. Contracts will also include a ''just cause'' provision stating that managers must provide specific reasons before disciplining or firing employees.\par \par \uc2 ''I am elated that we have such a strong contract for our members now and such a strong contract to build on in future negotiations,'' Natalie Meade, a New Yorker fact checker and the unit chair of The New Yorker Union, said in an interview on Wednesday. She added that the union had been able to ''break the curse of stagnant wages'' at the publication.\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast, which also publishes Vogue, Vanity Fair and Wired, among other publications, said the company was pleased to have reached an agreement with the unions. The ''new executive leadership team has implemented equitable compensation and inclusive benefits standards across our work force,'' she added. ''These standards are now reflected in our agreement with union employees.''\par \par \uc2 Roger J. Lynch joined the company as its chief executive in 2019. He leads Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast with Ms. Wintour, the longtime editor of Vogue, who is also Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast's worldwide chief content officer.\par \par \uc2 The New Yorker Union formed in 2018. It was the \uc2 first union\uc2  in the 96-year history of the magazine that has the monocled dandy Eustace Tilley as its mascot.\par \par \uc2 The New Yorker, which won six prizes at the National Magazine Awards last week, voluntarily recognized the union early on, but negotiations with Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast leaders moved slowly.\par \par \uc2 Last week, The New Yorker Union unveiled a website including the statement that it was ''on the verge of a strike.'' On June 8, roughly 100 people protested outside the home of Ms. Wintour, whose wide-ranging role at Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast does not include oversight of The New Yorker. The New Yorker has been run by the editor David Remnick since 1998.\par \par \uc2 Rachel Abrams contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Rachel Abrams contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 The New Yorker Union had said last week that it was ''on the verge of a strike.'' (PHOTOGRAPH BY John Taggart for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210617eh6h0004j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
