\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Many Workers Need More Than Skills to Fill Jobs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Steve Lohr \par \uc2 1,593 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 June 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Helping people land good jobs with career paths takes more than skills training, labor experts say. Coaching, mentoring and other assistance are also needed.\par \par \uc2 Rachelle Katchenago of Menasha, Wis., lost her job as a call-center contract worker last year after the pandemic lockdowns took hold. No one was hiring.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Katchenago, 37, eventually heard of career courses, paid for by \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  as part of a Covid relief program. She plunged in, learning online customer service and sales skills, hoping to improve her employment prospects.\par \par \uc2 But it turned out that the technical skills, in the free classes from Microsoft's LinkedIn job listing and training service, were not the only thing she picked up. The Wisconsin group helping her with the courses also provided career guidance, moral support, tips on r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 writing and interviews, and job contacts.\par \par \uc2 In November, Ms. Katchenago was hired as a customer service specialist at a thriving start-up based in Madison, Wis. She is making more money than in the past and she has a full-time job with a career path.\par \par \uc2 ''Without that support,'' she said, ''I would have been at a loss.''\par \par \uc2 The nation's jobs market stands at a somewhat puzzling juncture. There are still 7.6 million fewer people working than before the pandemic. But job openings are at record high levels, too. Some employers are even turning to incentives beyond money, like food and tuition for family members, to lure workers.\par \par \uc2 The reasons so many jobs remain unfilled are matters of vigorous and often partisan debate. Some people, including those in the Biden administration, say lingering health concerns and child care duties are holding workers back. Some conservatives argue that the extra unemployment benefits approved by Congress have lowered people's appetite to find a job.\par \par \uc2 But labor experts and some large employers point to another issue: the so-called last-mile problem. It is one thing to have in-demand skills, they say. But good jobs also require ''soft skills'' like teamwork, communication and the willingness to learn new things. And then, those freshly skilled people need to be matched to local job opportunities.\par \par \uc2 Research has shown the effectiveness of programs that are local, personal and focused on addressing the last mile of the labor market. President Biden has endorsed the model. The administration's jobs plan calls for billions in work force training including funds for ''wraparound services'' like counseling.\par \par \uc2 ''It's about seeing connections and building confidence,'' said Beth Cobert, chief operating officer of the \uc2 Markle Foundation\uc2 , which supports programs to broaden job opportunities. ''And you need people to do this, to help close that gap.''\par \par \uc2 Real progress in closing the last-mile gap could potentially benefit millions of Americans, economists say. Navigating the unfamiliar complexity of the labor market can be particularly difficult for the two-thirds of the nation's workers without four-year college degrees, with low incomes and people of color.\par \par \uc2 The comprehensive training approach has been refined at successful nonprofits that have spent decades dedicated to lifting people into the middle class, including Year Up, Per Scholas and Goodwill. They arrange for assistance with housing, transportation and child care for students, when needed. And they have strong links with local employers, tailoring their programs to hiring needs.\par \par \uc2 To streamline such work, the Center for Workforce and Economic Opportunity at the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Atlanta\uc2  is building metro area websites and developing programs to help career coaches across the country pinpoint local job openings and relevant training. The websites rely on data from Emsi and Burning Glass Technologies, labor market research firms.\par \par \uc2 The project is part of an initiative coordinated by the \uc2 Markle Foundation\uc2 , which includes corporations, nonprofits and civic groups. Its goal is to improve the livelihoods of unemployed and low-income workers as the economy recovers.\par \par \uc2 ''We need to really scale up what works well to bridge the social capital and networking gap,'' said Stuart Andreason, director of the Center for Workforce and Economic Opportunity.\par \par \uc2 Two tech giants, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2 , have learned last-mile lessons in their skills-training outreach programs.\par \par \uc2 In its pandemic program, begun a year ago this month, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  included $20 million to support last-mile organizations in the United States and abroad. Of the millions of people who have started the free courses, about 17 percent were trained through these local organizations. But they accounted for more than half of those who completed the coursework for certificates in job categories like customer service specialist, project manager and data analyst.\par \par \uc2 That evidence, according to \uc2 Brad Smith\uc2 , president of Microsoft, makes the case for the Biden administration's plan to invest more in job counseling by nonprofits, community colleges and local governments.\par \par \uc2 ''It's not enough to open the door to digital courses,'' Mr. Smith said. ''You need funding to provide people with mentoring and coaching. That's critical.''\par \par \uc2 In 2018, \uc2 Google\uc2  began offering a certificate course aimed at preparing people for entry-level tech support jobs.\par \par \uc2 Of the thousands of graduates since then, 59 percent do not have four-year college degrees and 46 percent previously had incomes in the bottom third of Americans.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  has also given out more than 20,000 scholarships to job seekers, made through last-mile nonprofits.\par \par \uc2 This year, \uc2 Google\uc2  started three more certificate programs in project management, user experience design and data analysis. Along with tech support, there are nearly 1.3 million job openings in those four occupations in America. \uc2 Google\uc2  announced it will grant 100,000 more scholarships over the next three years.\par \par \uc2 ''If your goal is economic mobility for folks, you have to do a lot more than create certificates,'' said Lisa Gevelber, vice president of Grow with \uc2 Google\uc2 , which includes company's skills training initiative.\par \par \uc2 One big nonprofit \uc2 Google\uc2  supports is \uc2 Goodwill Industries\uc2 . The group is best known for its stores that sell donated goods, but it also operates more than 600 career centers across the country. In the last few years, Goodwill locations, with support from \uc2 Google\uc2 , have offered courses in basic computer skills, as well as the professional track, tech support certificate course.\par \par \uc2 Chelsea Rucker had been living in a shelter for women in Nashville, Tenn., with her two young daughters. She managed to get a temporary job as an office worker at the local Goodwill center, and while there, learned of the \uc2 Google\uc2  tech support course.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Rucker, 33, started on the self-paced course, but made little progress at first. A turning point, she said, came when a manager from a nearby \uc2 Google\uc2  data center came to the Goodwill offices, described work at \uc2 Google\uc2  and job openings.\par \par \uc2 The connection made, Ms. Rucker could see a path to opportunity and her motivation kicked in. She began getting up in the predawn hours to do the \uc2 Google\uc2  course work before her daughters awakened. She was hired by \uc2 Google\uc2  in January 2019.\par \par \uc2 Today, Ms. Rucker has paid off old debts and bought a three-bedroom house on an acre of land. She has even founded a local nonprofit to assist families affected by incarceration -- partly inspired, she said, by Goodwill.\par \par \uc2 The organization that helped Ms. Katchenago, gener8tor Upskilling, began in August with 19 students in northeast Wisconsin, and has added others in communities in Indiana, Virginia and Wyoming, with 167 graduates so far.\par \par \uc2 Further expansion is planned. The program hopes to produce 600 or more graduates this year, said Joe Kirgues, its co-founder.\par \par \uc2 Learning the skills needed in a field, Mr. Kirgues said, has proved to be not so difficult for most people. So the focus has increasingly been on a ''whole person approach'' to preparing someone for a new job, he said, coaching, encouraging, sometimes arranging transportation or shopping for new clothes for a job interview.\par \par \uc2 By design, gener8tor Upskilling caters to relatively disadvantaged job seekers. About 40 percent of its students are minorities or veterans, and 56 percent are women.\par \par \uc2 The venture is paid based largely on graduation and job placement rates. The graduation rate is nearly 80 percent and job placement is more than 50 percent -- and rising, as the job market improves.\par \par \uc2 For Ms. Katchenago, things have certainly improved. With a full-time job, she no longer worries about being out of work after contracts expire every three to six months. Her current salary is about $30,000, which is $5,000 more than she made in past jobs. ''It's a blessing for stability,'' she said. ''I can breathe.''\par \par \uc2 In the customer service department at EatStreet, a meal delivery service that caters mainly to independent restaurants in midsize Midwestern cities, Ms. Katchenago has learned new skills like using \uc2 Salesforce\uc2  software. She sees steps on a career ladder. She has just taken on new duties as an ''implementation specialist,'' assisting restaurants adopt and use the company's online service.\par \par \uc2 At EatStreet, workers also move from customer service up to sales or data analyst roles, said Matt Howard, the company's chief executive.\par \par \uc2 ''There's a clear path for her,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Rachelle Katchenago lost her contract job last year. She now has a full-time job. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KEVIN MIYAZAKI FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B1); At Per Scholas in the Bronx, students take part in comprehensive job training, which includes teaching ''soft skills'' like teamwork and communication. (PHOTOGRAPH BY CHANG W. LEE/THE NEW YORK TIMES); Rachelle Katchenago with her 16-year-old son, Jonathan Mentzel. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KEVIN MIYAZAKI FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B3) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210614eh6e0004x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
