\par\par \uc2 Metropolitan Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Here to Help; How to Take Your Work Outside\b0\par\par\uc2 339 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 June 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 If you're working from home and lucky enough to have outdoor space, you can take your work outside. The key is planning for nature's surprises. Here are some tips for making your outdoor office comfortable. Melanie Pinola\par \par \uc2 STEP UP YOUR WI-FI\par \par \uc2 Even a good wireless router can have difficulty sending signals through walls. Install a Wi-Fi mesh-networking kit to expand your network's range. Wirecutter's pick: the Asus ZenWiFi AC (CT8).\par \par \uc2 IMPROVE YOUR FURNITURE\par \par \uc2 A lumbar-support pillow can help if you're working from a patio chair. And a seat cushion can make the worst plastic, wood or metal chair feel better than an economy-class airline seat. Wirecutter's pick: the Purple Double Seat Cushion.\par \par \uc2 CUT DOWN ON GLARE\par \par \uc2 Direct sunlight on your laptop screen causes a glare, and blazing hot temperatures are bad for your computer. Work under a tree, beneath an overhang or in the shadow of a patio umbrella.\par \par \uc2 PROTECT YOUR SKIN\par \par \uc2 Even if you're working under a canopy, indirect sunlight can reach you. Don't forget to protect yourself from damaging UV rays with a brimmed hat, good sunscreen and sunglasses. Wirecutter's pick for face sunscreen: Supergoop Play Everyday Lotion SPF 50 with Sunflower Extract.\par \par \uc2 MAKE CHARGING EASIER\par \par \uc2 If you don't have convenient outdoor power plugs, you can get a lightweight portable power station. Or set up some outdoor lights that end in an open outlet, which can charge your computer and beautify your space.\par \par \uc2 KEEP THE AIR MOVING\par \par \uc2 Use a house fan to keep yourself cool -- and to repel mosquitoes. A fan can also function as a white noise machine if your outdoor oasis is besieged with the sounds of urban life. Wirecutter's pick: Vornado 630 Medium Air Circulator.\par \par \uc2 Melanie Pinola is a senior staff writer for Wirecutter, a product recommendation site owned by The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . Read reviews and advice at nytimes.com/wirecutter.\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY KERKEZ/ISTOCK) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210606eh660005m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
