\par\par \uc2 THE HUNT\par \uc2 Real Estate Desk; SECTRE\par \b \uc2 Looking Beyond Their Comfort Zone and Finding a Happy Surprise\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joyce Cohen \par \uc2 825 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 8\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The first apartment that Kelsey and Dean Dimitruk rented together was on the Upper West Side, where they paid a little less than $3,000 for a one-bedroom in the West 90s.\par \par \uc2 ''We were very excited,'' Mr. Dimitruk said. ''There was an elevator and laundry in the building.''\par \par \uc2 Two years ago, after their marriage, the couple went in search of more space.\par \par \uc2 So they headed north to Hudson Heights, where their two-bedroom, one-bathroom apartment in a prewar condominium had a stacked washer and dryer. The apartment rented for $2,650 a month, and they found the benefits to be worth the added distance. ''This opened our eyes to areas outside of our comfort zone in Manhattan,'' said Mr. Dimitruk, 31.\par \par \uc2 It also allowed them to try out a less expensive area before making a purchase. ''I could see myself buying and not feel like I'm missing out if I'm not in the middle of everything,'' said Mrs. Dimitruk, 30.\par \par \uc2 The couple, who met as students at Emerson College in Boston, rode the A express train to their jobs downtown. (Mr. Dimitruk works in television production, and Mrs. Dimitruk in digital media.) They were planning for a baby, and wanted to buy a sunny two-bedroom, two-bathroom home with a view. Because they were working remotely during the pandemic, they craved a balcony.\par \par \uc2 Their budget was up to about $800,000, although Mrs. Dimitruk was eager to keep the purchase price low. ''I prefer to be living well below our means than within them,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 During the summer, they contacted Brittanie C. Bowers of Brown Harris Stevens, the listing agent for their Upper West Side place. She showed them a two-bedroom, two-bathroom unit in a Hudson Heights co-op building, with a balcony and treetop-level street views. The asking price was $780,000, with monthly maintenance in the $1,300s.\par \par \uc2 ''It had all of their criteria but was on the top of their budget,'' Ms. Bowers said. ''They would be a little stretched.''\par \par \uc2 The Dimitruks were having trouble finding two-bedroom units with a second bathroom. So Ms. Bowers took them up to the Bronx, where their money would go further. They spent a day touring nearly a dozen places in South Riverdale.\par \par \uc2 Among their options:\par \par \uc2 1. A Renovated Two-Bedroom\par \par \uc2 This recently renovated unit was about 1,000 square feet, on a high floor in a 1958 building, with an open living area and a balcony. It had one bathroom, but a second could be carved from the nook off the main space. The price was $585,000, with maintenance in the $1,300s.\par \par \uc2 2. A Corner Two-Bedroom\par \par \uc2 This corner apartment in a 1962 building, with two bathrooms and a balcony, was about 1,300 square feet and in rundown condition. It was priced at $675,000, and the maintenance in the $1,700s included utilities and cable TV.\par \par \uc2 3. A Top-Floor Two-Bedroom\par \par \uc2 This top-floor apartment in a 1967 building was about 1,400 square feet, with a balcony, a large living-and-dining area and a walk-in closet. The fixtures were dated, and the unit was said to need T.L.C. The price was $590,000, with maintenance in the $1,600s.\par \par \uc2 Their Choice\par \par \uc2 By day's end, the Dimitruks had made a decision: They took the first place they saw in Riverdale -- the renovated unit -- once they learned they could add a second bathroom.\par \par \uc2 ''We walked in, and the view was out of my dreams,'' Mr. Dimitruk said. ''The balcony was bigger than what I was expecting from the pictures. This one was the one to beat.''\par \par \uc2 The other options, despite being bigger and having a second bathroom, needed work that they felt would require too much money and effort.\par \par \uc2 The couple paid the asking price, arrived in the fall and immediately started planning the second bathroom. They visited a similar unit where the owners had converted the same space.\par \par \uc2 Then they learned that Mrs. Dimitruk was pregnant and decided that the time wasn't right for such a big project.\par \par \uc2 The two revel in their panoramic river view and their balcony, bundling up to use it in cool weather. ''The balcony is calming when you work from home and don't leave the whole day,'' Mrs. Dimitruk said.\par \par \uc2 The Dimitruks didn't set out to live in Riverdale, but they don't really miss being in the thick of the city. Compared with their old neighborhoods, ''maybe this one didn't have as many awesome restaurants, but that is something we didn't know yet,'' Mr. Dimitruk said. ''The value was so much more here.''\par \par \uc2 Email thehunt@nytimes.com\par \par \uc2 The Buyers: Kelsey and Dean Dimitruk near their new building. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ROBERT PRESUTTI FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210509eh590003j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
